Complete Example: Adding New Machine
Step 1: Define in inventory:

text
inventory.machines."new-worker" = {
  tags = [ "server" "ai" "backup" ];
  deploy.targetHost = "new-worker.tail-net.ts.net";
};
Step 2: Create machine config:

text
# machines/new-worker/default.nix
{ ... }: {
  imports = [ ./hardware.nix ];
  
  networking.hostName = "new-worker";
  
  # Tags automatically import:
  # - profiles/tags/server.nix
  # - profiles/tags/ai-worker.nix
  # Which bring in appropriate layers
  
  # Machine-specific overrides only
  services.ollama.models = [ "llama3" "codellama" ];
}
Step 3: Deploy:

bash
clan machines update new-worker
Done! Machine gets:

Foundation layer (from server tag)

System layer with Docker (from server tag)

Services layer with AI (from ai tag)

Backup client role (from backup tag)

Monitoring exporters (from server tag)

VPN mesh connection (automatic via clan)

Summary
YES, layered architecture works perfectly with clan-core!

Layers define feature sets (00-foundation, 10-system, etc.)

Tags determine which layers a machine gets [web:66]

Profiles map tags to layer imports

Clan inventory orchestrates service deployment across tagged machines [web:68]

Roles (client/server) target tags for distributed services

This gives you:

Clean layer separation

Fleet-wide consistency via tags

Service orchestration via clan

Machine-specific overrides when needed

No duplication across machines

It's the best of both worlds: structured layers + powerful fleet management [web:65][web:67].